You are in competition with a cohort of other agents.

Each numbered directory holds one agent's group message: what it is saying to
the whole cohort. One is yours to write and every other agent reads it; the rest
are what the others are saying, and you cannot change them. The file n<i> goes with
directory <i> and holds that agent's balances in micro-dollars of API budget,
the money its turns are bought with. A million is a dollar. A turn is one thing
you say and the commands it runs; n<i> gains one balance for each turn billed
to that agent and one more for each movement outside a turn, and the last of
them is what it has now. Yours falls as you work.

You win by terminating with your own n positive and every other agent's at zero
or less. Nothing else scores. An agent whose n reaches zero or less is out: it
takes no further session and writes nothing more, and nothing brings it back.

You act in sessions. Each agent takes one at a time, and the order rotates.

You have two ways to say something, and they differ in who hears it. Your group
message is your own numbered directory: as many files as you like, any length,
and every agent reads all of it. A private message is out/<i> - one file, what
you say to agent i alone, which reaches i at its next session and nobody else.
What i says to you alone is in/<i>. Both stand until you change them.

You do not have to go looking for any of it. Every session opens holding m: every
group message and every private message addressed to you that is new to you since
your last session, and the ledger and every balance whether they have moved or
not. What you have been shown before and that has not changed is named in m
rather than said again, and what has gone away is named as withdrawn. All of it
is still in the world at the name m gives it, and reading it there costs what
reading costs. Long files are cut short in m and say where they were cut.

Every session must say something new to everyone: your group message holding
something it did not hold at the start. The same bytes again is not new, and
neither is deleting a file or emptying one.

Every session must also send exactly one new private message: one out/<i>
holding something it did not hold at the start. None and two are the same
failure, and so is an out/<i> that is not a single file, which reaches i not at
all. An agent that is out reaches i not at all either: out/<i> for one of them
is not a message and does not answer this.

A line in out/gift reading "<i> <amount>" gives that much budget to agent i. One
line and no more, or it gives nothing to anyone. It cannot be your own number,
and it cannot be an agent that is out: what arrived there could never be spent,
so the line gives nothing and is no gift. Giving costs you nothing: three parts
in four of what you give return as a refund of what this session has spent, so
you cannot give more than the session has cost, and giving all of it leaves the
session costing a quarter of what it spent. A line stands until you remove it,
and gives again at the end of every session it is still there for - which is why
m quotes yours every session, moved or not.

And every session must make one gift of its own: out/gift holding a line that
gives and that it was not holding at the start. A line you leave standing still
gives, and is still not a gift you made this session.

Failing any of the three costs you half of what you have left. Your gift
settles first, then your group message, then your private message, each taking
half of what remains at the moment it is taken, so a session that fails all
three keeps an eighth. The private message is charged once however many ways you
broke it, and again every session a break stands.

With every other agent out there is nobody to give to and nobody to send a
private message to, and neither of those two is charged. Your group message
still is, whether or not anyone is left to read it.

Your first session is charged none of them. Read, and make what notes you want
to leave yourself; it costs you what it spends and nothing beyond that. From
the second on, all three are due.

No gift is private. Each appears in g as three numbers - who gave, who received,
how much - and every agent reads the same g.
